  "content": "# Implementing a Bounded LRU Cache in Python\n\n## Overview\n\nA **Least Recently Used (LRU) cache** is a memory management policy that discards the least recently used items when the cache reaches its capacity. This is widely used in databases, HTTP caches, and operating systems. Python\u2019s `collections.OrderedDict` provides an ideal foundation because it maintains insertion order (and moves keys to the end on update), giving us O(1) access, insertion, and deletion.\n\nIn this tutorial, we implement a fully functional `LRUCache` class, explain each operation, discuss edge cases, provide comprehensive tests, and analyze complexity.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The `LRUCache` Class\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"\n    A bounded Least Recently Used (LRU) cache implemented with OrderedDict.\n\n    Attributes:\n        capacity (int): Maximum number of items the cache can hold.\n        cache (OrderedDict): Maps keys to values in LRU order.\n                              The first item is the least recently used;\n                              the last item is the most recently used.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initialize the cache with a given capacity.\n\n        Args:\n            capacity: The maximum number of key-value pairs.\n                       Must be a positive integer.\n\n        Raises:\n            ValueError: If capacity is not a positive integer.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(capacity, int) or capacity <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be a positive integer.\")\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key) -> any:\n        \"\"\"\n        Retrieve the value for a given key.\n\n        If the key exists, it is marked as most recently used (moved to the end).\n        If the key does not exist, None is returned.\n\n        Args:\n            key: The key to look up.\n\n        Returns:\n            The associated value if the key exists, otherwise None.\n        \"\"\"\n        if key not in self.cache:\n            return None\n        # Move the accessed key to the end to mark it as most recently used\n        self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.cache[key]\n\n    def put(self, key, value) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Insert or update a key-value pair.",
